# Copy Style Rules

These apply to any user-visible text you write or edit on this site: page
copy, meta descriptions, button labels, badge labels, email templates
(`app/api/contact/route.ts`), and article content (`lib/articles.ts`). They
do not apply to code comments (`{/* ... */}`, `// ...`) or internal
docstrings, which are never rendered to a visitor.

## No em dashes
Never use "—" in user-visible copy. It reads as an AI-writing tell. Rewrite
with punctuation a human editor would actually choose:
- **Sentence-level aside** → period or restructure the sentence
  ("...decision liability — in an acquisition..." → "...decision liability,
  whether in an acquisition...")
- **Internal clause** → comma, semicolon, or "and"/"but" depending on the
  relationship
- **Parenthetical list** → actual parentheses
  ("companies — capital, technologies —" → "(companies, capital,
  technologies)")
- **Title/tagline separator** (page titles, meta descriptions) → colon
  ("Casimir Systems — AI..." → "Casimir Systems: AI...")
- **Compact UI label pairs** (badges, status chips) → the site's existing
  middle-dot convention (`·`), e.g. `"D2P2 Submitted · Space Force"`

A full site-wide cleanup pass happened in July 2026 (see git history:
"Remove all em dashes from site copy" and the follow-up opportunity-scanner
sweep). Don't reintroduce the pattern in new content.

## Aspirational language only
This is a small company competing for government contracts and pursuing
certifications it doesn't hold yet. Never claim something as achieved,
awarded, or certified unless a human has explicitly confirmed it happened.
Use aspirational framing instead:
- "pursuing," "targeting," "eligible for," "proposal submitted," "award
  decision pending" — NOT "awarded," "active contract," "certified"
- Example that was live and had to be corrected: "Active Phase II Award"
  became "D2P2 Submitted" once it was clear no award had been made.
- Example: "HUBZone-Eligible" is correct today (principal office qualifies);
  "HUBZone Certified" would be false until SBA actually certifies the
  company. Don't "round up" a pending/eligible status to a completed one for
  better-sounding copy.
- If you're unsure whether a claim is confirmed, ask, or default to the more
  conservative aspirational phrasing.

## No generic AI-writing filler
Avoid: "leverage," "seamless," "cutting-edge," "unparalleled," "delve,"
"furthermore," "moreover," "boasts," "game-changer," "revolutionize,"
"elevate," "harness the power of," "unlock the potential," "in today's
fast-paced landscape," "it's not just X, it's Y." Write plainly and
specifically instead of reaching for these stock phrases.
